<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The capability ASAuthorizationController needs, written out and deliberately
  not yet wired into the build.

  SignInWithApplePlugin.swift compiles and links without this. What it cannot do
  without it is succeed: an authorization request from a binary whose
  provisioning profile does not carry com.apple.developer.applesignin comes back
  as an error, which the plugin reports as a sign-in that could not be
  completed. So this is the runtime half of the capability, and the file exists
  so that turning it on later is one build setting rather than a hunt for the
  right key.

  It is not referenced from project.pbxproj on purpose. Setting
  CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS to this file makes the App target fail to build for
  anyone whose signing identity cannot grant the capability, and a free personal
  team cannot — Sign in with Apple is a paid Apple Developer Program capability.
  Wiring it today would therefore break the iOS build that currently works, in
  exchange for enabling a flow that has no credentials behind it anyway.

  When there is a membership, three things happen together and none of them
  works alone: tick Sign in with Apple on the App ID in the developer console,
  add the capability to the App target in Xcode (which sets
  CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS to this path and regenerates the profile), and set the
  four APPLE_SIGNIN_* secrets on the Worker.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
